### Reflection Support
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=reflection></a>

Reflective programming[^reflection] is the ability of a process to examine, introspect, and modify its own structure and behavior.

Features like [CLI help]({{_}}/cli-help) and [automatically generated API docs]({{_}}/api) require that `compose.mk` has at least *some* ability to parse its own contents.  

And if we need that kind of ability, why not allow for parsing of other Makefiles?  As another applied example, see the [zero-config TUI documentation]({{_}}/zero-config-tuis/), which makes use of {{site.api_link('include',mkdocs,arg='fpath')}}.  

Most of the meta-tooling that's available is somewhere in the [`mk.*` target namespace]({{_}}/api#api-mk).

#### Makefile Metadata 
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

For metadata parsing, you can use the `mk.parse/<fname>` target, and get back targets, target chains, target docs, etc.  

{{site.api_link_many(['mk.parse'],mkdocs)}}

For testing, there is a super lightweight `demos/no-includes.mk` Makefile included in the repository for situations like this. Like the name suggests, it doesn't actually include or use `compose.mk` in any way.

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.parse/demos/no-include.mk | jq 'del(.__main__)'
```
```json {.cli_output}
{{bash("./compose.mk mk.parse/demos/no-include.mk | jq 'del(.__main__)'")}}
```

*Under the hood, the heavy lifting for Makefile-parsing uses the native `mk.parse` backend (awk + jq over `make`'s own database), built into `compose.mk`.*


#### Validation and Planning
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Validation of external Makefiles is used as part of code-generation and [compilation]({{_}}/cmk/compiler), but it's also exposed for general use.

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.validate/Makefile mk.validate/demos/no-include.mk
```
```bash {.cli_output}
✱ mk.validate .. Makefile ✔
✱ mk.validate .. demos/no-include.mk ✔
```

#### Reading Defines
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Reading `define ... endef` blocks from makefiles is important for several different `compose.mk` features that rely on embedded data.  And crucially, the contents of those blocks **must not be mangled**, escaped, interpolated, etc.

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.def.read/pygmentize
```
```Dockerfile {.language-dockerfile .cli_output}
FROM ${IMG_ALPINE_BASE:-alpine:3.21.2}
RUN apk add -q --update py3-pygments
```

This feature is the foundation that all sorts of other stuff is built on, including support for [polyglots]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) and [the embedded tui]({{_}}/embedded-tui) as well as [inlined Dockerfiles]({{_}}/raw-docker), etc.

{{site.api_link_many(['mk.def'],mkdocs)}}

#### Grabbing Symbols
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Reading defines is the most common need, but you can also get the expanded value of arbitrary symbols using `mk.get`.  This is sometimes useful for previewing what macros actually do.. for example to show the implementation of the `stream.nl.to.comma` macro:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.get/stream.nl.to.comma
( cat /dev/stdin | awk 'BEGIN{ORS=","} {print}' | sed 's/,$//' )
```

For a more practical example, see [this section]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming/#about-dialects) of the compiler docs.

#### Pattern-Matching on Targets
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

There are various ways to enumerate available targets for the current runtime, either restricting it to targets defined in the local file, or adding targets from `include` as well.  

One nice thing about this is the ability to easily dispatch *all targets matching a given pattern*.  

{{site.embed_demo('demos/flux.star.mk', github=github)}}

See also the API docs for {{site.api_link('flux.star',mkdocs,arg='pattern')}}.

#### Dynamic Includes and Targets
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

To a certain extent, `compose.mk` can do *runtime* modification of `make` itself (or at least simulate it).  Under the hood, this works by using code-generation to create a new Makefile just in time, and then [turning execution over to it]({{_}}/signals/).  Also somewhat related is [the interpreter]({{_}}/signals/#inheritance-interpreters), and [the CMK compiler]({{_}}/cmk/compiler).

**Create a new target dynamically.** Here's an example that creates a new target on the fly, assigning it to an existing target that simply returns success.  After the new target is defined, it can be used on the same command line.

```bash {.cli_example}
./compose.mk mk.let/foo:flux.ok foo
```
```bash {.cli_output}
 ✱ mk.let // foo:flux.ok // Generating code .. ./.tmp.5xdPP3008 
 Φ flux.ok // succeeding as requested! 
```

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

**Dynamic includes.** Suppose you want to script with `compose.mk`, but the project makefile does not `include compose.mk`.  You can mix together automation APIs like this:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk include/demos/no-include.mk flux.timer/clean
```
``` bash {.cli_output}
cleaning
Φ flux.timer // clean // done in 0.000000000s 
```

What just happened?  Pulling apart this command, note that `compose.mk` offers no `clean` target, and `demos/no-include.mk` has no `flux.timer` target.  

The command above is similar to `make -f .. -f .. ` but retains the compose.mk API and signal-handling.  Or to put it another way, it executes the following Makefile just in time, then proxies commands to it:

```Makefile {.snippet} {.language-Makefile}
include compose.mk
include demos/no-include.mk
```

#### Forks, Guests, and Payloads
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

!!! related_links "See also"
    For bundling a lot of files and/or additional data, see also the [packaging demo]({{_}}/demos/packaging), which provides similar capabilities via self-extracting archives.  This method is more lightweight and tactical.

For a few specific use-cases, `compose.mk` has the ability to fork it's own source code.  Helpers related to this are in the `lang.src.fork` namespace.  All unary targets require a filename, and nullary ones assume that input is on stdin.

{{site.api_link_many(['lang.src.fork'],mkdocs)}}

In each case, targets return the source code that results from *merging the given file-contents with the current source-context.*  Merges happen in specific sections, and each section has different semantics.

**Guests** 
: are expected to be Makefile source code.  It's appended after the main body of source, similar to [dynamic includes](#dynamic-includes-and-targets).
: &nbsp;

**Payloads** 
: are expected to be raw data, and this input is always wrapped with `define PAYLOAD .. endef`.  This could be JSON, [polyglot source code]({{_}}/demos/polyglots), or anything else.
: &nbsp;

**Services** 
: are expected to be docker-compose.  This input is always wrapped with `define SERVICES .. endef`, and afterwards [`_compose.import.string`]({{_}}/bridge/#inlined-embedded-from-string) is used, bringing all that [service scaffolding]({{_}}/bridge) into the root namespace.

You can use overrides in these sections together to **bundle projects as tools**, or to **bundle extensions + core libraries + related containers into a single script**.

The typical use-case looks like this:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Create a quick example Makefile
$ echo 'hello-world:; echo hello world' > tmp.mk

# Construct a new script, combining a Makefile and a compose file
$ bin=./tool.mk ./compose.mk lang.src.fork/tmp.mk,demos/data/docker-compose.yml

# Result is executable, has access to project automation library, 
# and all the tool containers have target scaffolding
$ ./tool.mk hello-world
$ ./tool.mk debian.shell
$ ./tool.mk debian.dispatch/flux.ok
```

Above, `lang.src.fork` is just helper that uses the `lang.src.fork.*` primitives, but you can also use them piecewise:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Starting with `compose.mk` as a base, create 
# a "forked" script that wraps the project makefile
$ cat tmp.mk | ./compose.mk lang.src.fork.guest > fork1.mk
$ chmod +x fork1.mk

# Use the fork to generate the next fork,
# this time setting default services from the docker-compose file
$ cat demos/data/docker-compose.yml | ./fork1.mk lang.src.fork.services > fork2.mk

# Cleanup, rename result
$ rm fork1.mk
$ chmod +x fork2.mk
$ mv fork2.mk tool.mk
```

Afterwards, the `tool.mk` result is the same as before.  See the related {{macros.repo_link("demos/payload.mk", github=github)}} tests for more details.

